UNEP/GRID-Warsaw Centre was established on 17 September 1991 pursuant to the official agreement signed between the Director of the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) and the Polish Government (Ministry of the Environment, Forestry, and Natural Resources), as a centre working within the Global Resource Information Database (GRID) network. Formally, the Centre has been established in the structures of the National Foundation for Environmental Protection (hence, it has the non-profit status).
The Centre actively co-operates with self-government authorities, educational institutions and private companies pursuing corporate social responsibility (CSR) principles in a wide range of projects devoted to effective environmental management, application of remote sensing, geographic information systems (GIS), and environmental education.
Specific areas of expertise and activity of the UNEP/GRID-Warsaw Centre entail the following:
&middot; geoinformation and remote sensing: analyses of spatial data on environmental and socio-economic phenomena and processes; geodatabase development and maintenance (incl. data harmonisation according to the INSPIRE implementing rules); development and market application of GIS solutions and geoportals; practical implementation of result of scientific discovery related to novel geoinformation tools and methods;
&middot; environmental governance and reporting: thematic mapping and geovisualisation, incl. 3D modelling; development of GIS and ICT applications to support decision making and spatial planning by public administration units; use of mobile GIS-based tools and techniques to support monitoring and rapid mapping activities; implementation of the INSPIRE Directive in Poland;
&middot; capacity building: specialized trainings, workshops, seminars, conferences; e-Learning courses; educational materials and publications &ndash; targeted primarily at public administration bodies and related to the use of remote sensing tools, GIS tools and applications (with focus on open source solutions), spatial data for environmental management, spatial planning, crisis management, etc.
&middot; environmental campaigns, environmental education, public participation: using modern multimedia and other ICT solutions; youth education and awareness building on issues related to environment/nature protection, community involvement, building ICT literacy; fostering public and inter-institutional consultations, e.g. in spatial planning procedures, aided by geoinformation tools and methods (geo-consultations); citizen science; CSR and sustainable business practices;

The ENTENTE pour la for&ecirc;t m&eacute;diterran&eacute;enne, known as ENTENTE VALABRE, is an independent public administrative body that works closely with the Ministries of the Interior, Ecological Transition and Territorial Cohesion (MTECT) and Agriculture. As a key interdepartmental player at both institutional and territorial level, VALABRE's main challenge is to protect people and areas from major natural hazards.
Bringing together 31 local authorities, including 15 d&eacute;partements, 15 Services D&eacute;partementaux d'Incendie et de Secours (SDIS) and the Collectivit&eacute; Territoriale de Corse, its missions focus on the pooling of skills and resources, as well as consultation with its partners, with the aim of optimising the management of major risks. The organisation is divided into 4 departments:
- The Information and Prevention of Major Risks Department ;
- The Civil Security Training School (ECASC);
- The Test and Research Centre (CEREN);
- The Innovation and New Technologies Centre (PONT).

The PONT offers its members a suite of technological solutions covering all the needs associated with managing forest fires and fires in natural areas, from prevention to operational response, feedback and continuous improvement. VALABRE is strategically positioned as a provider of shared operational solutions, with a coherent vision at territorial level. The COP CRIMSON, used by the Centre Op&eacute;rationnel de Gestion Interminist&eacute;rielle des Crises (COGIC), the 7 Centres Op&eacute;rationnels de Zone de d&eacute;fense (COZ) and more than 50 fired departments, continues to be deployed in France (SDIS, departmental councils, local authorities) and internationally. The CRIMSON tool also provides real-time information to the ORSEC and SYNAPSE national crisis management platforms, making VALABRE the only French entity authorised to supply these Ministry of the Interior networks. Once operational, the NEXIS project, which aims to pool the Alert Management Systems (SGA) and Operational Management Systems (SGO) used in the SDIS, will also incorporate a CRIMSON gateway.
The PONT also keeps a proactive watch on innovative technologies, with the aim of enhancing its services to end users (emergency services, civil protection, inter-municipalities, regional authorities, etc.). For example, PONT was commissioned by MTECT to carry out the PANOPT&Egrave;S study, the aim of which is to deploy a system for the early detection (<5 minutes) of all forest fires and fires in natural areas on a national (metropolitan) scale, and to monitor them in near-real time (burnt areas, fire fronts, fire breaks, hot spots, etc.). This early detection is in line with the national operational doctrine of "massive attack on incipient fires".
